{
  "oxlint": "1.60.0",
  "oxc": "b0817ae6459a91be4ad98cfe1006cb5656de9994",
  "notes": {
    "eslint/no-const-assign": "Twelve upstream cases declare `using` at the top level of a script. Oxlint rejects them as a syntax error and Kessel's parser accepts them, so they cannot be compared.",
    "eslint/no-eval": "Sixteen cases use `this.eval` under allowIndirect: false. Kessel does not decide whether `this` is the global object there. The default configuration agrees on every case.",
    "eslint/no-new-wrappers": "Oxlint treats every `new String()` as the global once the file has any unresolved `String`. In `const a = new String('bar'); { const String = CustomString; const b = new String('foo'); }` it also reports the second call. Kessel resolves each reference: executing that code shows the inner call constructs CustomString, not a wrapper.",
    "typescript/no-misused-new": "`type T = { constructor(): void }` is not reported because the lint walk does not visit type literals. `declare abstract class C { new(): C }` is reported by oxlint only.",
    "unicorn/no-empty-file": "A file that holds only `/* eslint-disable no-empty-file */` is reported: Kessel applies a block disable from the end of its comment and this diagnostic starts at offset 0.",
    "unicorn/no-unnecessary-await": "`await (<></>)` at the top level of a .tsx file without imports is a script to Kessel, which then reads `await` as an identifier and stops with a parse error."
  },
